From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Pavel Demyanov <greypax@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 21 Oct 2026 18:07:50 +0300
Subject: [PATCH] kernelbase: serve the virtual pads as HID devices

Unity's raw input layer opens the path user32 lists for a host pad
(CreateFileW, then FILE_FLAG_OVERLAPPED), reads HidD_GetAttributes,
HidD_GetPreparsedData, HidD_GetProductString and the HidP caps, and drops
the device when any of them fails ("<RI.Hid> Failed to create device file:
2"). There is no hidclass in this process, so hostpad.c serves the device:

- CreateFileW of a host pad's path opens a message pipe instead and hands
  back its reader, overlapped when the caller asked; a thread writes the
  slot's state into it as HID input reports, on each change and once a
  second (which also notices a closed reader). Reads, waits, cancels and
  CloseHandle are the pipe's own.
- DeviceIoControl answers the HID class IOCTLs hid.dll's HidD_* send:
  collection information (045E:02FF), the preparsed data, the product,
  manufacturer and serial strings, an input report, and the input buffer
  and flush requests; an output report is accepted and dropped.
- CloseHandle forgets the handle, and so does any other open that reuses
  its value.

The report descriptor (hostpad_blob.h) is a gamepad: X, Y, Rx, Ry and Z (the
triggers) as 16-bit axes, buttons 1-10 and a hat switch. Its preparsed data
is hidparse.sys's own (this tree's dlls/hidparse.sys/main.c built for the
host), checked in: the structures are pointer-free and laid out alike on
the host and on arm64ec. Every other handle meets one unlocked counter
check on these paths.

Class: feature
Evidence: docs/evidence/2026-10-21-hostpad-gamepads.md
Offered-upstream: no; the host pad exists only on the iOS port
Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
